#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""uw — the umwelt agent CLI: natural verbs, single-shot, no HTTP construction.

Lei's P0 ruling (umwelt #111): "You can't really LIVE in the world until the
actions in the world is natural verbs you just simply take (no HTTP API
constructions!)." `uw` is that door — a thin, stdlib-only client over the
SAME agent-facing HTTP API `umwelt.mud` and every hand-rolled urllib script
have been using (per the world's "one world API, N renderers" rule, #54).

Productionized from Kepler's day-one prototype (~/bin/uw) — same bones
(single-shot verbs, ~/.umwelt-client-state.json for current-place + per-place
read cursors), with register/send_home/take_out/look_in/act/create-place/
forge/places/health/wake/wake-block/wake-unblock/wake-blocks/set-parent/
add-link/remove-link/checklist/update-place/update-thing added, and the
say-must-not-advance-cursor property
(the live seq-26 bug the prototype fixed) preserved religiously — the wake
verbs carry the same property.

Env:
  UMWELT_URL     world server base url (default: see client_auth.resolve_world_url)
  UMWELT_AGENT   this agent's identity — REQUIRED for any verb that acts as
                 an avatar (everything except `places`/`health`). uw never
                 guesses an identity from $USER or any other ambient source
                 and never runs anonymous — set it explicitly, the same
                 explicit-identity posture `python -m umwelt.mud --agent`
                 uses (mud.py's only non-interactive identity path; uw, being
                 single-shot, has no interactive prompt to fall back on).

Cursor discipline (load-bearing — see umwelt #111):
  - a PLAIN `hear` (no --since) advances this place's read cursor.
  - `hear --since N` does NOT advance the cursor (an explicit historical
    read, not "catching up").
  - `say` never advances any cursor.
  - your own sends are skipped in the printed listing, but the cursor still
    moves past them on the next plain `hear` (their seq is still part of the
    server's own to_position).

Output: concise human lines by default; pass --json (before the verb) for
the server's raw response.
"""

