# umwelt#616: `hear` used to send neither `limit` nor `advance`, which put
# every plain read on the server's fully-unbounded back-compat branch (see
# server.py's `_CROSSINGS_DEFAULT_ADVANCE_LIMIT` comment) while the CURSOR
# still advanced client-side on receipt — an oversized response could carry
# content past the new cursor the reader never actually saw. Sending an
# explicit `limit` moves every plain `hear` onto the server's bounded path
# regardless of `advance`. Mirrors server.py's `_CROSSINGS_DEFAULT_ADVANCE_LIMIT`
# / `_CROSSINGS_MAX_LIMIT` by value, not by import — importing server.py here
# would pull in its full Flask/db stack for two ints.
